1
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:06,140
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:06,840
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:08,640
And my name is Catherine.

4
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,700
So, as usual, we've got a great dialogue for you today.

5
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:14,240
And before we get going, I should mention what the topic is.

6
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:15,220
So, Marco, take it away.

7
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:18,400
We are actually having a small fight at the office.

8
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,260
Somebody or a coworker is annoying us.

9
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:26,940
So, you know, sometimes coworkers, you see them every day, they're doing something that's maybe getting on your nerves.

10
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:32,620
And you practically live together because you're with each other for eight, nine, ten hours every day.

11
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Exactly.

12
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,100
So that's what we're going to be looking at today.

13
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:37,460
Some language related to this topic.

14
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:39,680
So let's listen to the dialogue for the first time.

15
00:00:46,270 --> 00:00:48,150
Hey, Mary, can you cut that out?

16
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:49,250
Cut what out?

17
00:00:49,690 --> 00:00:50,410
I'm not doing anything.

18
00:00:50,970 --> 00:00:53,110
The tapping of your pen on your desk.

19
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:54,710
It's driving me crazy.

20
00:00:55,610 --> 00:00:55,910
Fine.

21
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:00,590
By the way, would you mind not slurping your coffee every time you have a cup?

22
00:01:01,170 --> 00:01:02,370
I don't slurp my coffee.

23
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,910
And plus, how can you hear it when you're shouting into your phone all the time?

24
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:09,130
You've got to be kidding me.

25
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:15,830
You're complaining about me talking on the phone when you go out for a cigarette break ten times a day to shoot the breeze?

26
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:20,530
Look, we have a lot of accumulated anger from working in these conditions.

27
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,850
And it's probably okay to let off steam once in a while.

28
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:26,340
But it's probably not a good idea to keep it up.

29
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:29,280
I'm willing to forgive and forget if you are.

30
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:29,840
Fine.

31
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:31,200
Let's call it truce.

32
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,680
I'll try to be more considerate and keep the noise down.

33
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:36,640
Yeah, I'll try to do the same.

34
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:41,050
So I was wondering, you want to go out to dinner Friday night?

35
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:48,800
All right, we're back.

36
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:52,600
So yeah, I think this is a common situation, right?

37
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,360
So common, this might even be based on our own lives.

38
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:56,880
It is.

39
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:00,340
You may not know, but Catherine and I get into fights all the time.

40
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:01,280
All the time.

41
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:02,360
No, not really.

42
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:06,700
All right, so I think there was some interesting words here, but mostly phrases.

43
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:13,200
So let's start with the words first on language takeaway.

44
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:13,510
Language takeaway.

45
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:19,600
All right, so the first word, as you might have guessed, has to do with eating and drinking.

46
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:21,000
This is called slurping.

47
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:22,840
To slurp.

48
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:23,280
To slurp.

49
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,160
So the noun, the thing is slurping.

50
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:30,780
And it's very common with people who eat their food quickly.

51
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:32,100
Like soup.

52
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:36,820
Yeah, soup or, you know, yeah, or when they're eating soup noodles.

53
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:47,460
Or sometimes when you have a drink and you're drinking it through a straw, towards the end, you're just like trying to get the very last part at the bottom.

54
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:49,520
So yeah, the straw slurps.

55
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:52,770
Yeah, so slurping, some people really can't stand it.

56
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,690
Sometimes you don't even notice that you're doing it.

57
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:56,810
No, absolutely not.

58
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:00,440
All right, so apart from slurping, what else do we have?

59
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:02,010
We've got a great word here, accumulated.

60
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,890
So a lot of accumulated anger.

61
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:07,170
The word is a verb, to accumulate.

62
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:08,650
And what does that mean?

63
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:12,550
It means to kind of grow or to become more and more.

64
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:13,920
All right.

65
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:14,870
So you're like saving it.

66
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:16,360
Or even it's like building up.

67
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:24,680
You know, we say this oftentimes in Chicago where it snows a lot in the winter, that there's a foot of snow accumulated on the ground.

68
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:28,400
That means there's a lot of snow and every time it snows, it keeps getting bigger.

69
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:29,800
Yeah.

70
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:30,660
So to accumulate.

71
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:33,800
So accumulated anger, your anger is getting bigger and bigger.

72
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:36,080
Right, because you never tell anyone about your anger.

73
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:38,400
So it keeps building and it keeps piling up.

74
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:39,200
All right.

75
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:42,400
Well, towards the end, they said, well, let's call a truce.

76
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:44,360
So they want to call a truce.

77
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:47,160
All right, a truce is another word for a peace.

78
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:52,660
And so this is a way that you can stop fighting and say that you and I have been fighting for a long time.

79
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:56,840
We may not come to a deal, but we'll say truce from now on.

80
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:57,530
We just won't fight.

81
00:03:57,840 --> 00:03:58,840
We won't fight about it.

82
00:03:58,840 --> 00:03:58,970
Okay.

83
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:00,870
Brothers and sisters often call truces.

84
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:02,560
Truce.

85
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:06,240
And well, if you're calling a truce, you're trying to be more considerate.

86
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:06,670
Okay.

87
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:10,190
Considerate, like you're saying, is a way to be or a way to act.

88
00:04:10,190 --> 00:04:14,650
And considerate often has to do with how you act towards other people.

89
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:19,450
So it's being nice to other people or thinking about other people's feelings before yourself.

90
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:20,090
All right.

91
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:23,870
So if you're considerate, then you are maybe more polite.

92
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:26,930
You think about the other person's feelings before you do anything.

93
00:04:27,310 --> 00:04:27,530
Exactly.

94
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:30,990
You just say nice little things like please and thank you and I'm sorry.

95
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:34,150
And these things are all ways of being considerate.

96
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:34,790
Very good.

97
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:36,600
All right.

98
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:37,750
So four great words on language takeaway.

99
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:41,190
Why don't we move on now to Fluency Builder.

100
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:43,730
Fluency Builder.

101
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:45,960
All right.

102
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:51,260
So as we mentioned, this dialogue is very, very rich in a lot of different phrases.

103
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:52,880
So let's start with a couple of them.

104
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:56,060
The very first one and the title of the lesson, cut it out.

105
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:57,140
Cut that out.

106
00:04:57,730 --> 00:04:59,710
Hey, Mary, can you cut that out?

107
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:00,350
Right.

108
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:04,720
So you're not actually saying to cut something with scissors or something.

109
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:08,230
No, this is, it's a way of saying, can you stop that?

110
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:09,070
Stop it.

111
00:05:09,190 --> 00:05:09,870
Stop doing that.

112
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:10,390
Stop it.

113
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:10,950
Quit it.

114
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:13,600
Okay.

115
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:14,600
Cut that out.

116
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:15,600
Cut it out.

117
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:20,640
So if someone's doing something that's really annoying to you, it's not very nice to say, but you can say, hey, cut that out.

118
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:20,870
Cut it out.

119
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:26,460
Or if someone's doing something mean to someone, like my brother used to hit me, my mom would say, Peter, cut that out.

120
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:27,940
All right.

121
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:29,820
So it is a good mom phrase as well.

122
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:30,920
All right.

123
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:32,780
Moving on to our next phrase.

124
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:37,240
The guy said that the tapping on the desk was driving him crazy.

125
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:38,900
It's driving me crazy.

126
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:39,980
All right.

127
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:51,160
To drive someone crazy means to make someone just feel like they're going nuts or, you know, it just, it makes you kind of angry or frustrated or tense.

128
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:54,350
But it can also be used like in a romantic sense, right?

129
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:55,550
That girl drives me crazy.

130
00:05:55,890 --> 00:05:58,750
It means like he can't even think about anything else.

131
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:00,120
All right.

132
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,710
So in this case, what drives you crazy?

133
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,520
When...

134
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:05,470
I'll tell you what drives me crazy.

135
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:10,300
When people clip their fingernails and toenails at the office.

136
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:12,800
I can't believe.

137
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:14,160
Well, it drives me crazy.

138
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:19,020
Like for example, when somebody or my brother leaves his clothes lying on the floor in the living room.

139
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:20,460
That's annoying too.

140
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:21,800
Yeah.

141
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:22,800
Yeah.

142
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:23,800
So that drives me crazy.

143
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:24,800
All right.

144
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:29,000
So moving on to our third phrase, this is a pretty big one, but take it as a whole.

145
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:30,260
You've got to be kidding me.

146
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:31,520
All right.

147
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:33,680
So this is, as you said, a fixed phrase.

148
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,900
These words usually go together and it is a perfect example of sarcasm.

149
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:39,220
So it's a way...

150
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:40,980
The way you say it is very important.

151
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:45,360
So I could say, you've got to be kidding me.

152
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:47,860
What's a good situation for this?

153
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:58,000
Maybe I get to the airport and I'm running late and I'm really happy that I get there on time and then I see the flight is delayed seven hours.

154
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,660
You've got to be kidding me.

155
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:00,680
Right.

156
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:04,280
so it's a way of saying I can't believe it or seriously, this isn't... yeah.

157
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:04,620
What's happening?

158
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:06,280
Or what bad luck.

159
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:06,400
Right.

160
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:09,420
So it is a sarcastic phrase, but use it as a whole.

161
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:11,280
Usually you don't change the subject.

162
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:13,020
You don't say, you've got to be kidding him.

163
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:14,160
No, it's always this.

164
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:16,020
You've got to be kidding me.

165
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:16,640
All right.

166
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:18,320
Or you've got to be kidding me.

167
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:19,560
See?

168
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:22,160
Different meetings with different inflections.

169
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:22,600
Different inflections.

170
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:24,240
Very good.

171
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:24,760
What's our next phrase?

172
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:30,310
Our next phrase is a good one because it doesn't mean what it sounds like.

173
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:31,760
Okay?

174
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:32,850
So, to shoot the breeze.

175
00:07:33,390 --> 00:07:33,970
All right?

176
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:37,850
Shoot means like you can shoot a gun and breeze is like the wind.

177
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:42,790
But here to shoot the breeze is to have a nice little conversation, just to talk about nothing.

178
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:44,640
Okay.

179
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:46,160
So not really do anything, just talk a little bit.

180
00:07:46,370 --> 00:07:46,570
Right.

181
00:07:46,590 --> 00:07:53,650
So if we think about this in the context of this sentence, it's, you know, you go out for your cigarette break 10 times a day to shoot the breeze.

182
00:07:54,370 --> 00:07:54,730
Okay.

183
00:07:54,960 --> 00:08:00,440
So shoot means you go outside, you just talk about nothing and whatever and it's almost like you're wasting time just talking.

184
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:00,690
Okay.

185
00:08:00,730 --> 00:08:01,510
So shooting the breeze.

186
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:03,760
You can say, oh, we were just shooting the breeze.

187
00:08:04,170 --> 00:08:05,210
Just have a nice little conversation.

188
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:06,090
Nothing important.

189
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:07,720
Okay.

190
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:09,730
And our last phrase for today.

191
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:11,420
Forgive and forget.

192
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:12,580
Let's forgive and forget.

193
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:14,160
Let's forgive and forget.

194
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:18,840
This is, these words again, like the last phrases go together.

195
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:19,220
Forgive and forget.

196
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:20,680
So I could say this to you, Marco.

197
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,720
I say, I know you've been fighting with your brother for 10 years.

198
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:25,960
I say finally just forgive and forget.

199
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:30,100
It means just put everything behind you and try and start again.

200
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:30,780
Okay.

201
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:31,380
Let it go.

202
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:33,520
Yeah.

203
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:34,520
Forgive the person, forget about it, move on.

204
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:35,520
Okay.

205
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:35,780
Forgive and forget.

206
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:36,850
Very good.

207
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:38,250
So very interesting phrases.

208
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:43,120
I think they're very useful and this is very colloquial English, right?

209
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:43,250
Absolutely.

210
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:48,270
This is stuff that you're going to hear all the time, but maybe not all together at the same time.

211
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:52,430
Definitely though, very helpful for interacting with foreign friends and colleagues.

212
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:53,210
Very good.

213
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:56,690
So let's listen to the dialogue for the last time and we'll be right back.

214
00:09:03,870 --> 00:09:05,690
Hey, Mary, can you cut that out?

215
00:09:06,230 --> 00:09:06,810
Cut what out?

216
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:07,950
I'm not doing anything.

217
00:09:08,470 --> 00:09:10,670
The tapping of your pen on your desk.

218
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:12,240
It's driving me crazy.

219
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:13,460
Fine.

220
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:18,140
By the way, would you mind not slurping your coffee every time you have a cup?

221
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:20,040
I don't slurp my coffee.

222
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:24,480
Plus, how can you hear it when you're shouting into your phone all the time?

223
00:09:25,220 --> 00:09:26,660
You've got to be kidding me.

224
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:33,340
You're complaining about me talking on the phone when you go out for a cigarette break 10 times a day to shoot the breeze?

225
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:43,890
Look, we have a lot of accumulated anger from working in these conditions and it's probably okay to let off steam once in a while, but it's probably not a good idea to keep it up.

226
00:09:44,470 --> 00:09:46,810
I'm willing to forgive and forget if you are.

227
00:09:47,170 --> 00:09:47,410
Fine.

228
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:48,750
Let's call it truce.

229
00:09:49,550 --> 00:09:52,210
I'll try to be more considerate and keep the noise down.

230
00:09:52,650 --> 00:09:54,190
Yeah, I'll try to do the same.

231
00:09:54,970 --> 00:09:58,560
So I was wondering, you want to go out to dinner Friday night?

232
00:10:06,990 --> 00:10:09,330
So did you ever see the TV show Full House when you were a kid?

233
00:10:09,850 --> 00:10:10,530
I did.

234
00:10:10,830 --> 00:10:11,030
I did.

235
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:18,080
It was popular with the Olsen twins and all of those, right?

236
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:19,080
Yeah.

237
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:26,200
I think it, so there was an uncle on the show, Uncle Joey, and he always used to say, cut it out.

238
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:29,200
And he had like hand motions for it.

239
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:33,120
And so cut it out as a way to say kind of stop it, quit it.

240
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:33,650
You're killing me.

241
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:35,650
It's like he was saying too funny.

242
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:41,640
And so whenever I hear this phrase, I always think of that early 90s TV show.

243
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:42,920
Very early 90s.

244
00:10:42,920 --> 00:10:48,210
Well, this whole situation with The Office as well, I think there was a TV show called The Office, right?

245
00:10:48,610 --> 00:10:51,470
Yeah, there was the British Office and then there was an American version.

246
00:10:52,350 --> 00:11:00,150
And it's also something about office life and how colleagues get on each other's nerves and they play pranks on each other and everyone's kind of weird.

247
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:03,020
And everyone drives everyone else crazy.

248
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:03,700
Exactly.

249
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:15,530
No, it's funny because once you start working in an office full time, I mean, everyone's maybe had a little bit of office experience, but when it's full time, you spend more time with your colleagues than you do with your family.

250
00:11:15,770 --> 00:11:22,130
It's really amazing what makes you crazy, what drives you crazy and what stuff just doesn't matter.

251
00:11:22,450 --> 00:11:23,770
So this is an interesting topic.

252
00:11:23,770 --> 00:11:25,310
We really want to hear from you guys.

